00:00:00.380 --> 00:00:05.269 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  so i want you to think back  on if you've received an email from a  political campaign  that read something like this  or this  or this  so this is what my inbox looked like  last week  and i'll come clean  having worked on a campaign i've sent  thousands of these emails  a belated apology if any of you watching  are from the state of new hampshire  now it may come as a surprise to hear  that emails  are a major part of how candidates reach  voters  however i'm sure it's no surprise to  hear that they're incredibly ineffective  click-through rates are abysmal  and studies have shown that excess  outreach actually reduces intent to vote  it's counterproductive  however on a campaign  there's no time to experiment  no time to consider new strategies  we do what's been done before  in fact  over the last two decades  the playbook for driving voter turnout  has not significantly changed  we've just gone from door knocking  to bot worthy digital spam  and yet those of us working on campaigns  are baffled  given the billions  spent on elections every four years  how do we not have a better system for  voter outreach  or even more broadly  why does it seem that the issues  plaguing our democracy  are unending  the lack of secure voting  disinformation  partisan districting  i could go on  so this is what i'm here to talk about  today  one  what's wrong with our democracy  two  why democracy entrepreneurship might be  the solution  and three  how we can fund and support this work  so let's start with the first question  what's wrong with our democracy  you know most americans once  acknowledged a few universal truths  sun will rise  tom brady will win another super bowl  and american democracy will persist  now fast forward to 2022  tom brady is retired  and according to a recent poll  64 percent of americans  believe that u.s democracy is in crisis  now this is a harrowing fact  our democracy is the system by which we  peacefully transition power  and hold leaders accountable  it is because of the resiliency of our  democracy that we have free and fair  markets  and why the us dollar  is the world's reserve currency  without a trusted government  we cannot fight corruption  advocate for human rights  or pursue justice  these are the promises that a healthy  democracy delivers  thus  it is baffling to think that we are not  only ignoring improvement  but we're actively moving backwards  the last time trust levels in the  american democratic process were this  low  was in the 1850s in the lead up to the  civil war  the non-partisan electoral integrity  project ranks the united states second  to last among liberal democracies  and 57 overall  in administering trusted and fair  elections we must completely reject the  notion that our democracy will magically  bumble into better times  it won't without active intervention  so  with the laundry list of issues in tow  you'd guess a country like the united  states so focused on relentless  improvement would throw its full weight  into revitalizing democracy  however government isn't incentivized to  tackle democracy reform  and i could think of a few reasons why  one  it's hard to use the system  to change the system  politicians aren't incentivized to upend  the process that put them in power  two dependency corruption  the constant need to fundraise for  elections  often drives partisanship over progress  three  polarization  entrenched polarization makes any chance  at pipe at passing bipartisan electoral  reform  difficult  and four  the scale of government  even donors who are passionate about  democracy  think they can just support part reform  candidates  however  reform friendly politicians cannot  unilaterally make progress  so because government isn't incentivized  to tackle this issue  we need to do what america does best  turn to our entrepreneurs  in this case i'm talking about democracy  entrepreneurs  democracy entrepreneurs represent a  legion of passionate and patriotic  leaders  they include technologists  they include research organizations  they include community organizers  and they include advocacy groups  they drive innovation and change in a  space that needs it  it is because of democracy entrepreneurs  at ballot ready that we have access to  state and local level ballot information  with two clicks  it's because of democracy entrepreneurs  at rock the vote that we can register to  vote online  it's because of democracy entrepreneurs  at politifact that we have near instant  fact checking  of politicians  whereas private sector entrepreneurs  innovate in antiquated industries  and social entrepreneurs take on issues  tackling societal safety  democracy entrepreneurs  are working on solutions to build a  government of the people  by the people  and for the people  their key distinction is that they're  not chasing a financial exit  they're often non-profits or social  welfare organizations  so we've laid out the issues with  democracy  and identified who is best positioned to  fix them  so now to address the last question  how we can fund and support this work  to best illustrate this i'll share a  story about a time that i worked for a  democracy entrepreneur  at voterev we used behavioral science  and organizing expertise to drive  bipartisan voter turnout  it was september of 2020 and our  organization was unstoppable  we'd raised over 10 million dollars  we'd recently doubled our head count  and we were actively turning out  thousands of early voters  now fast forward to february 2021  exactly one month after the georgia  runoffs  we were no longer receiving  contributions  and over 50 percent of my colleagues  have been laid off  now the core issue  is that infusions of political dollars  are painfully tied to elections  every four years candidates and  political groups are flooded with  billions and like clockwork  post-elections  those dollars dry up  no matter how successful we were in 2020  we couldn't raise our series b  we had to downsize just like hundreds of  other democracy ventures  and just like that  progress was ground to a halt  and our democracy remained stuck  waiting for the inevitable  another election  for those funds to come flooding back in  so this is the fate of democracy  entrepreneurship today  blessed and damned by the whims of  donors  tied to the shoestrings of political  contributions  and wholly dependent on the cyclicality  of our elections  so  how do we fix this  the first part of the solution  involves reallocating political dollars  to fund democracy entrepreneurs  every four years donors give over 10  billion dollars to campaigns and  political groups  and only a fraction of a percent of that  money goes towards strengthening  democracy  just like we do with science and  infrastructure we need to make more  investments in democracy and we need to  make them more strategically and longer  term the second part of the solution  involves supporting this work  this is where you all come in  as future investors and operators we  need to empower this work by building  accelerators and incubators  by cementing democracy innovation as a  part of corporate social responsibility  by providing technical resources to  founders  and by evangelizing to build the  movement  i am calling for a pro-mass democracy  mobilization event  it's about time we recognize the service  of democracy entrepreneurs and flood  them with funding and support  we do this with the goal of inviting all  americans to co-create our nation  by giving them a fair say in the forces  that govern their lives  and i'll end with this  it's not about which side wins  what matters most  is for american democracy to win  [Music]

